<MakerList items={makers} />

<h2 id="differences-heading">Five Differences to Compare</h2>
<h3>Phase offered</h3>
<p>A supplier that builds only dilute phase will quote dilute phase. If your material is abrasive or breaks easily, include at least one supplier that builds dense phase so you see both designs priced. The guide explains <a href="/conveyor-types/pneumatic-conveyors/#phase-heading">dilute phase vs. dense phase</a> in detail.</p>
<h3>Air source</h3>
<p>VAC-U-MAX, Volkmann, and Hapman list vacuum only. Vacuum fits several pickup points feeding one destination and keeps leaks inward. Pressure fits one pickup feeding several destinations and reaches longer distances. Nol-Tec, Cyclonaire, Coperion, Flexicon, and Kice state both. See <a href="/conveyor-types/vacuum-conveyors/">vacuum conveyors</a> for where vacuum fits.</p>
<h3>Semi-dense phase</h3>
<p>Cyclonaire names semi-dense phase as a third category between dilute and dense. Other suppliers fold it into one of the two. When two quotes use different phase names, compare the conveying velocity and the air pressure, not the label.</p>
<h3>Material testing</h3>
<p>Pneumatic systems are sized from how a specific material behaves in a pipe. Most listed companies state that they run a test lab, but the published scale varies widely. The useful questions are whether the test uses your material, at full scale, and over a route close to yours.</p>
<h3>Combustible dust</h3>
<p>Suppliers describe explosion protection in different terms. Volkmann and Flexicon cite ATEX, which is a European Union scheme. Hapman cites a pressure-rated receiver for use with venting or suppression. Others say nothing on their conveying pages. In the United States the governing standard is <a href="https://www.nfpa.org/codes-and-standards/nfpa-660-standard-development/660" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NFPA 660</a>, which combines the former NFPA 61, 484, 652, 654, 655, and 664. ATEX certification of a conveyor does not by itself show that an installation meets NFPA 660. <a href="https://www.osha.gov/combustible-dust" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OSHA</a> publishes its own combustible dust guidance.</p>

<SpecChecklist id="rfq-heading" title="Pneumatic Conveying RFQ Checklist" items={[
  ['Material', 'Name, bulk density, particle size range, moisture content, and whether it is abrasive, fragile, sticky, or hygroscopic.'],
  ['Rate', 'Pounds or kilograms per hour, and whether transfer is continuous or in batches.'],
  ['Route', 'Horizontal distance, vertical lift, and number of bends.'],
  ['Pickups and destinations', 'How many of each, and what equipment feeds and receives the material.'],
  ['Product damage', 'How much breakage or segregation the process can tolerate.'],
  ['Combustibility', 'Dust test data if available, and the status of your dust hazard analysis.'],
  ['Sanitary requirements', 'Cleaning method, changeover frequency, and any FDA, USDA, or 3-A requirement.'],
  ['Utilities', 'Compressed air capacity and pressure, available power, and space for a blower or vacuum pump.'],
  ['Testing', 'Whether the supplier will test your material, at what scale, and whether the result backs a performance guarantee.'],
  ['Scope', 'Who supplies controls, installation, and commissioning.'],
]} />

